# Goal 10: Fix cproj / cprojf — wrong infinity handling

## Functions
`cproj` (double), `cprojf` (float)

## Source files
- C (double): /home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/libm/complexd/cprojd.c
- C (float): /home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/libm/complexf/cprojf.c
- Rust: /home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/newexp/rust-s3/src/complexd.rs (cproj), /home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/newexp/rust-s3/src/complexf.rs (cprojf)

## Problem
When input has an infinite component, cproj should return (inf, copysign(0, imag)):
- cproj(inf, -inf) = (inf, inf) Rust vs (inf, -0) C
- cproj(NaN, inf) = (inf, inf) Rust vs (inf, 0) C
- cprojf shows same pattern

The Rust implementation doesn't handle the infinity projection correctly — it should replace the imaginary part with copysign(0, imag) when either component is infinite.

## What needs to change
Compare Rust cproj/cprojf against C cprojd.c/cprojf.c. The infinity detection and result construction needs to match: when isinf(real) || isinf(imag), return CMPLX(INFINITY, copysign(0.0, imag)).

## Success Criteria
- `cproj (inf,-inf)` = `inf,-0x0p+0` (bitwise exact)
- `cproj (nan,inf)` = `inf,0x0p+0` (bitwise exact)
- `cprojf (inf,-inf)` = `inf,-0x0p+0` (bitwise exact)
- `cprojf (nan,inf)` = `inf,0x0p+0` (bitwise exact)
